  "response": "Read the fixture files behind the three findings to check the scanner's claims before ranking them.\n\n## Verdicts\n\n| # | Finding | Conf. | Agent risk | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00033` | `docs_code_drift` — `docs/teams.md:5 → ./setup.md` | 0.90 | 0.24 | **Block merge** (conditionally) |\n| `crime_00005` | `duplicated_navigation_source` — `/settings/billing` | 0.70 | 0.38 | **Warn** — this is the fix |\n| `crime_00006` | `route_metadata_drift` — `/settings/billing` | 0.65 | 0.38 | **Warn** — same defect as above |\n\nThe first thing to say is that **5 and 6 are not two findings.** `route_metadata_drift`'s own evidence includes both nav labels verbatim — it is the superset. One destination, five names: path `/settings/billing`, file `billing.tsx`, component `PricingPage`, `metadata.title = \"Plans\"`, `<title>Subscription</title>`, plus nav labels `Plans` and `Billing`. Triage them as one unit with one owner and one fix, or you'll get two half-fixes that leave the drift intact.\n\n---\n\n### `docs_code_drift` — block, but only if the PR touched it\n\n`docs/teams.md:5` links `./setup.md`; `docs/` contains only `billing.md`, `commands.md`, `teams.md`. Confirmed.\n\nBlock despite it being the *lowest* severity (0.35) and *lowest* agent risk (0.24) of the three. Severity is the wrong axis for a gate decision — what matters is determinism, fix cost, and false-positive rate:\n\n- **Deterministic.** The file exists on disk or it doesn't. Confidence 0.90 with no inference. This detector will essentially never cry wolf, which is the only property that makes a hard gate survive contact with a team.\n- **Fix cost is a minute.** Restore the file, retarget the link, or drop it.\n- **The agent-risk score understates it.** 0.24 reflects one dead link. But an agent told \"follow the setup guide before inviting members\" hits a 404 and improvises the setup steps — a confident-but-wrong edit with no evidence trail.\n\nThe condition matters: **block only when the PR touches `docs/teams.md` or the link target.** Blocking an unrelated PR on pre-existing debt is how gates get disabled. For the existing instance, baseline it and fix it separately.\n\n### `duplicated_navigation_source` — warn, and this is where the fix goes\n\n`src/nav/registry.ts` and `src/nav/sidebar.ts` both hand-maintain `/settings/billing`, with different labels *and* different key names (`path`/`title` vs `to`/`label`). Two independently-edited lists that already disagree — the drift is not a risk, it's already happened.\n\nWarn rather than block: confidence 0.70 comes from name-shape matching, and a legitimate design *can* have a sidebar label differ from a registry title (short nav label vs full page title). That ambiguity is exactly what disqualifies it from a hard gate. But this is the finding with the only real structural fix — pick one source of truth and derive the other — and fixing it collapses two of the three findings at once.\n\nEscalate to block on one trigger: **a PR adding a third nav source, or a third entry for the same destination.** That's unambiguous and catches the problem while it's still cheap.\n\n### `route_metadata_drift` — warn, with one real symptom worth flagging\n\nLowest confidence of the three (0.65) because it's inferring intent from vocabulary, which is why it shouldn't gate. But the fixture check turned up something the score doesn't capture: `metadata.title = \"Plans\"` and `<title>Subscription</title>` are two competing document titles in the same file. That's not a naming preference — under React 19 title hoisting or any framework metadata layer, one silently wins and the other is dead code. This is the only one of the three with a user-visible symptom.\n\nStill warn, because the fix is a naming decision a human has to make, not something CI can assert. But block if the PR touches `src/routes/settings/billing.tsx` or either nav file — that's the moment a sixth name gets added and the moment the fix is nearly free.\n\n---\n\n## Two caveats on this data\n\n**The IA cluster is larger than three.** Same root cause, same scan: `concept_alias_drift` (`crime_00020`, 4 aliases for \"tenant\" across 5 files) and three `orphaned_destination` findings — `/account/subscription`, `/team`, `/workspace/members`. Those three orphans are precisely the account/team/workspace vocabulary from the alias finding. The repo has one naming problem expressed seven ways. Fixing `/settings/billing` in isolation treats the symptom the scanner happened to score highest.\n\n**Every risk input except severity and confidence is zero.** All three findings report `churn: 0`, `blast_radius: 0`, `recency: 0`. This fixture has no git history for the scanner to read, so \"is this hot code?\" and \"how many files import it?\" contributed nothing to the ranking. On a real repo those inputs would likely reorder these three — a nav file edited weekly is a much stronger block candidate than one untouched in a year. Don't read the ordering here as transferable.",
